WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:11.140
In March 1936, Dorothea Lange was driving through Nipomo, California on her way home

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.000
after a long day of work for the Resettlement Administration,

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.240
a New Deal agency created by FDR

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.580
to relocate struggling families onto land developed by the government.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:22.820
Lange was a photographer

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.100
For the last fifteen years or so, she'd made her living taking portraits of the San Fransisco elite.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:33.080
But after the Great Depression hit, she left her studio and began to document the effects of the crisis

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:34.760
on residents of the city.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.240
On seeing those photographs, Roy Stryker immediately hired her to work for the government

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:41.420
on a project that would involve

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.420
documenting poor rural workers

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.400
in a propaganda effort to elicit political support

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.960
for government aid.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.220
Lange was riding home having finished her work,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.500
film ready to be sent back to Washington,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.100
when she passed a pea-picker's camp.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.000
"Pea-picker" was a name given to poor migrants from Eastern cities and towns

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:03.460
who moved west, often to California, looking for farm work during the Depression.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.800
At this camp, an early-morning frost had killed the

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.880
crops so some 3,000 people were left in
their makeshift homes with no work, some

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:17.549
starving, but Lange passed them by,
exhausted from the day's work. It was 20

00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:22.650
more miles before she turned around. She
didn't stay at the camp long - in about

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.610
10 minutes she would be back on the
road again - but not before taking one of

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:31.820
the most iconic pictures in the history
of photography. Here's how it happened.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.439
On arriving at the camp, Lange is
immediately drawn, "as if by a magnet," she

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:42.060
said, to a family huddled under a tent.
This is likely a test shot. There are a

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.930
lot of things that make this composition
unpersuasive. Only the child in the

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.829
middle, for example, is offering her face
to the camera. The oldest daughter on the

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:54.540
chair doesn't seem to notice the camera
at all, and the mother turns away, clearly

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:59.100
defensive, not wanting her picture taken.
So Lange talks to them as she's learned

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.299
to do after working this beat for a long
time, and she helps the family arrange

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.400
themselves. This composition is
definitely more pleasing than the last,

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.420
but something's still not quite right,
and it has to do with the eldest

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.599
daughter, here. Remember, these photos have a

00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:19.620
purpose: to evoke sympathy from voters
that would translate into political

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.910
support. As James Curtis points out,
having an older daughter, as well as

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:28.319
three younger ones, could suggest to some
that this woman wasn't taking the right

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:32.880
precautions and family planning, and was
less deserving of support - or, in other

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:38.220
words, less like them. So Lange cuts her
out. She captures - or stages - a breast

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.120
feeding scene, playing on the religious
symbolism of the nursing Madonna, but the

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.630
picture may have been too extreme for
viewers of that time. What's more, it was

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:49.680
clearly making the mother uncomfortable,
and Lange is starting to realize that she

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.799
is the key to the scene. Specifically, her
face. So she alters the angle, asks one of

00:02:55.799 --> 00:02:59.850
the children to come back in, and you can
see the woman easing up, but the older

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:04.140
child looks a bit awkward and the post
is blocking part of the baby's head, so

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.120
Lange tries again, this time having the
great idea to include the trunk at an

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.440
angle in the foreground with the empty
pie tin, compelling imagery of the

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.420
family's struggle and nomadic lifestyle.
The child looks wistful, the baby is

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.829
sleeping, and the mother has the right
expression, too. This is an extraordinary

00:03:22.829 --> 00:03:27.810
photograph. You can see Lange coming to
it through a series of adjustments. It

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:32.250
achieves the objectives of her project
while being aesthetically powerful.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:37.680
It's exactly what Ron Stryker would have
loved to see, but Lange thought she could do one better.

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.239
This is the photograph you
probably know. I don't think you can

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:48.660
really appreciate it until you
understand the 10 minutes that came

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:52.590
before. A lesser photographer would have
milked the children's faces for their

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:56.609
sympathetic potential. Lange even tried
to do that herself, but by the time she

00:03:56.609 --> 00:04:01.440
arrives here she knows exactly what the
focus needs to be. The daughters' faces

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.340
turned behind their mother's shoulders
communicates that message of family -

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:09.810
a family of the right size, mind you.
Without taking away from the central

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:14.940
face, or the eyes, which seem at last to
let down their guard as they search the

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:18.660
distance in worry. The face is so
important that Lange has the woman put

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:22.860
her hand on her chin so that her arm
points to it. It's a technique that she's

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:27.000
used before. Anything that took attention
away from the face would hurt the

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.110
power of the image, which also explains
why Lange had a darkroom assistant

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:35.790
remove the woman's other thumb, here,
where she was holding the post so that

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:40.380
she could keep her other hand to her
chin. All this manipulation, of course,

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:45.000
speaks to the fact that there is very
little spontaneous in this iconic image

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.470
of so-called "documentary photography."
It's supposed to communicate a candid

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:53.910
truth about the moment, but this is
anything but candid. Whether that

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:58.920
diminishes its power is up to you. For me,
being able to actually see the steps of

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:04.020
Lange's craft enhances her work, it gives
the photograph a third dimension. Almost

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.860
as soon as it was developed, this photo
took on a life of its own. After it was

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.550
published in a San Francisco newspaper,
the government sent 20,000 pounds of

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:14.280
food to the migrant camp at Nipomo.
Stryker

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:18.150
fell in love with it and sent it all
across the country, after which it came

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:22.680
to define the Depression in the American
consciousness, and on a deeper level

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:28.440
it became the archetypal image of
struggling families in any era. A second

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:34.440
thought, a skilled photographer, a mother,
her kids, and 10 minutes in 1936 that

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:40.340
will live forever. Next time you have an
instinct to turn around, listen to it.

